==English==

===Etymology===
{{uder|en|la|-}} ''[[nil#Latin|nīl]]'' ‘nothing’, ''[[desperandum|dēspērandum]]'', gerund of ''[[desperare|dēspērāre]]'' ‘despair’, as used in ''Nil desperandum Teucro duce et auspice Teucro'' ‘no need to despair with Teucer as your leader and Teucer to protect you’, in [[w:Horace|Horace]], ''Odes''

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/nɪl dɛspəˈɹændəm/}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj|head=nil desperandum}}

# Do not [[despair]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1745|author=Rapin de Thoyras & N. Tindal|title=The History of England, Volume XXII|publisher=James and John Knapton|location=London|page=407f
|passage=The Pretender's motto upon the colours and standards were, ''Dieu & mon Droit'', "God and my Right:" '''''Nil desperandum''' Christo duce & auspice Christo'', "I ought not to despair, since Christ is my guide and helper:"}}

==Latin==

===Phrase===
{{head|la|phrase|head=[[nīl]] [[dēspērandum]]}}

# nothing to be despaired of
#: ''Nil desperandum Teucro duce et auspice Teucro'' (there is nothing to despair about with Teucer as our leader and Teucer as our protector). &mdash; [[Horace]], Odes, I.vii.27 (translation Benham's Book of Quotations).
# never despair.
# I am not going to give up. Not for giving up, as in: this is not for giving up on.